{"end":728.93,"sent":[{"id":"h1_1","s":0.2,"e":2.389,"text":"Zeus swallowed his own wife."},{"id":"h1_2","s":2.609,"e":3.989,"text":"She was pregnant."},{"id":"h2_1","s":4.439,"e":6.375,"text":"He did it on purpose."},{"id":"h2_2","s":6.595,"e":9.685,"text":"And an ancient Greek poem tells us why."},{"id":"h3_1","s":10.135,"e":13.8,"text":"This was after Zeus had won a ten-year war."},{"id":"h3_2","s":14.02,"e":17.861,"text":"After he had thrown down a monster with a hundred heads."},{"id":"h3_3","s":18.081,"e":21.817,"text":"After the other gods had urged him to be their king."},{"id":"h4_1","s":22.317,"e":26.792,"text":"To see why, we have to go back to the first ruler of the sky."},{"id":"h4_2","s":27.012,"e":30.147,"text":"And to a warning he gave his own sons."},{"id":"h5_1","s":30.647,"e":34.66,"text":"On the way, we will find a stone that was fed to a god."},{"id":"h5_2","s":34.88,"e":39.574,"text":"And a painter who drew the monster very differently from the poet."},{"id":"a1_1","s":40.274,"e":44.645,"text":"The poem is the Theogony, by a Greek poet named Hesiod."},{"id":"a1_2","s":44.945,"e":49.236,"text":"Most scholars date it to about seven hundred B.C."},{"id":"a2_1","s":49.736,"e":53.101,"text":"The story of the world begins with Chaos."},{"id":"a2_2","s":53.401,"e":55.368,"text":"Then Gaia, the Earth."},{"id":"a2_3","s":55.668,"e":64.013,"text":"And from Gaia comes Ouranos, the Sky, made just as big as she is, so that he can cover her on every side."},{"id":"a3_1","s":64.513,"e":67.473,"text":"Gaia and Ouranos have children."},{"id":"a3_2","s":67.773,"e":70.061,"text":"Twelve of them are Titans."},{"id":"a3_3","s":70.361,"e":74.638,"text":"Three are Cyclopes, one-eyed makers of the thunderbolt."},{"id":"a3_4","s":74.938,"e":80.739,"text":"And three are Hundred-Handers, giants with a hundred arms and fifty heads each."},{"id":"a4_1","s":81.239,"e":85.28,"text":"Ouranos hates the three Hundred-Handers most of all."},{"id":"a4_2","s":85.58,"e":90.249,"text":"And he hides his children away inside Gaia, as each one is born."},{"id":"a5_1","s":90.749,"e":93.876,"text":"Gaia groans, cramped with her own children."},{"id":"a5_2","s":94.176,"e":100.478,"text":"So she makes a sickle out of grey flint, and asks her sons to use it on their father."},{"id":"a6_1","s":100.978,"e":103.439,"text":"They are all too scared to answer."},{"id":"a6_2","s":103.739,"e":105.004,"text":"All but one."},{"id":"a6_3","s":105.304,"e":108.266,"text":"Cronos, the youngest, the clever one."},{"id":"a7_1","s":108.766,"e":113.383,"text":"Gaia hides Cronos in ambush, and puts the sickle in his hand."},{"id":"a7_2","s":113.683,"e":116.983,"text":"When Ouranos comes at night, Cronos strikes."},{"id":"a7_3","s":117.283,"e":120.196,"text":"And Cronos takes his father's place."},{"id":"a8_1","s":120.696,"e":123.736,"text":"Ouranos has one thing left to say."},{"id":"a8_2","s":124.036,"e":127.552,"text":"He calls his sons Titans, as an insult."},{"id":"a8_3","s":127.852,"e":137.545,"text":"Hesiod says the word comes from a verb meaning to strain, because, Ouranos says, they strained too far, and did a terrible thing."},{"id":"a9_1","s":138.045,"e":141.839,"text":"And he promises that one day, someone will pay for it."},{"id":"a10_1","s":142.489,"e":143.823,"text":"Remember that."},{"id":"b1_1","s":145.723,"e":147.859,"text":"Cronos is king now."},{"id":"b1_2","s":148.159,"e":152.212,"text":"And he has learned a prophecy from Gaia and Ouranos."},{"id":"b1_3","s":152.512,"e":155.598,"text":"One day, his own son will overcome him."},{"id":"b2_1","s":156.098,"e":158.506,"text":"So Cronos makes a rule."},{"id":"b2_2","s":158.806,"e":164.124,"text":"Every child that his wife Rhea bears, he swallows as it is born."},{"id":"b3_1","s":164.624,"e":166.008,"text":"Five children."},{"id":"b3_2","s":166.458,"e":167.533,"text":"Hestia."},{"id":"b3_3","s":167.733,"e":168.862,"text":"Demeter."},{"id":"b3_4","s":169.062,"e":169.959,"text":"Hera."},{"id":"b3_5","s":170.159,"e":171.108,"text":"Hades."},{"id":"b3_6","s":171.308,"e":172.408,"text":"Poseidon."},{"id":"b4_1","s":172.608,"e":174.956,"text":"Rhea's grief never ends."},{"id":"b4_2","s":175.256,"e":182.946,"text":"When she is about to bear a sixth, she goes to her own parents, Gaia and Ouranos, and asks for a plan."},{"id":"b5_1","s":183.446,"e":185.134,"text":"They send her to Crete."},{"id":"b5_2","s":185.434,"e":190.453,"text":"In a cave on a mountain, Gaia takes the baby from her, and hides him."},{"id":"b6_1","s":190.953,"e":195.947,"text":"To Cronos, Gaia gives a great stone, wrapped in baby clothes."},{"id":"b6_2","s":196.247,"e":197.4,"text":"He takes it."},{"id":"b6_3","s":197.7,"e":199.034,"text":"He swallows it."},{"id":"b6_4","s":199.334,"e":201.265,"text":"And he never notices."},{"id":"b7_1","s":201.715,"e":203.638,"text":"That baby is Zeus."},{"id":"b7_2","s":203.938,"e":205.107,"text":"The youngest."},{"id":"b7_3","s":205.407,"e":207.636,"text":"The one who was not eaten."},{"id":"b8_1","s":208.136,"e":209.943,"text":"Hesiod skips ahead."},{"id":"b8_2","s":210.243,"e":212.082,"text":"The boy grows strong."},{"id":"b8_3","s":212.382,"e":216.005,"text":"Gaia tricks Cronos, and Zeus overpowers him."},{"id":"b8_4","s":216.305,"e":220.421,"text":"That is all the poem says about how the gods got free."},{"id":"b9_1","s":220.921,"e":223.555,"text":"Cronos throws them all back up."},{"id":"b9_2","s":223.855,"e":227.57,"text":"The stone comes first, because it was swallowed last."},{"id":"b10_1","s":228.07,"e":237.556,"text":"Zeus plants that stone in the ground at Pytho, another name for Delphi, below Mount Parnassus, so that people will see it and wonder."},{"id":"b11_1","s":238.056,"e":244.166,"text":"More than eight hundred years later, a Greek traveller named Pausanias visits Delphi."},{"id":"b11_2","s":244.466,"e":246.993,"text":"And he finds a small stone."},{"id":"b12_1","s":247.493,"e":251.809,"text":"Every day, he writes, the locals pour olive oil over it."},{"id":"b12_2","s":252.109,"e":255.12,"text":"At every feast, they lay raw wool on it."},{"id":"b12_3","s":255.42,"e":261.435,"text":"And some say this is the stone that was given to Cronos in place of his child."},{"id":"b13_1","s":261.935,"e":266.902,"text":"Zeus also frees his uncles, the Cyclopes, who have been chained up."},{"id":"b13_2","s":267.202,"e":271.229,"text":"They thank him with thunder, lightning, and the thunderbolt."},{"id":"b13_3","s":271.529,"e":274.942,"text":"Until then, Gaia had hidden the thunder away."},{"id":"c1_1","s":275.842,"e":277.154,"text":"Now the war."},{"id":"c1_2","s":277.454,"e":280.346,"text":"The Titans hold Mount Othrys."},{"id":"c1_3","s":280.646,"e":283.908,"text":"Zeus and the young gods hold Olympus."},{"id":"c2_1","s":284.408,"e":287.242,"text":"Hesiod does not say what started it."},{"id":"c2_2","s":287.542,"e":292.536,"text":"He only says they fight for ten full years, and neither side can win."},{"id":"c3_1","s":293.036,"e":295.098,"text":"Then Gaia speaks again."},{"id":"c3_2","s":295.398,"e":301.034,"text":"She tells the gods that with the three Hundred-Handers on their side, they will win."},{"id":"c3_3","s":301.334,"e":306.778,"text":"But the Hundred-Handers are still chained under the earth, where Ouranos put them."},{"id":"c4_1","s":307.278,"e":310.79,"text":"So Zeus and the gods bring them up to the light."},{"id":"c4_2","s":311.09,"e":314.094,"text":"Then they give them everything they need."},{"id":"c4_3","s":314.394,"e":318,"text":"Nectar and ambrosia, the food of the gods."},{"id":"c5_1","s":318.5,"e":320.331,"text":"Their spirit comes back."},{"id":"c5_2","s":320.631,"e":325.821,"text":"Zeus reminds them how he freed them, and asks them to show their strength."},{"id":"c5_3","s":326.121,"e":329.783,"text":"Cottus, one of the three, answers for all of them."},{"id":"c5_4","s":330.083,"e":331.323,"text":"They will fight."},{"id":"c6_1","s":331.823,"e":333.104,"text":"The sea rings."},{"id":"c6_2","s":333.404,"e":335.062,"text":"The earth crashes."},{"id":"c6_3","s":335.362,"e":343.682,"text":"Olympus shakes on its foundations, and the shock reaches all the way down to Tartarus, the pit beneath the world."},{"id":"c7_1","s":344.182,"e":346.845,"text":"And now Zeus stops holding back."},{"id":"c7_2","s":347.145,"e":350.537,"text":"He comes down from Olympus, hurling lightning."},{"id":"c7_3","s":350.837,"e":352.559,"text":"The forests crackle."},{"id":"c7_4","s":352.859,"e":354.797,"text":"The sea starts to boil."},{"id":"c7_5","s":355.097,"e":358.682,"text":"The heat, Hesiod says, seizes Chaos itself."},{"id":"c8_1","s":359.182,"e":364.347,"text":"The Hundred-Handers throw three hundred rocks, one on top of another."},{"id":"c8_2","s":364.647,"e":368.538,"text":"They bury the Titans beneath the earth, and bind them."},{"id":"c9_1","s":369.038,"e":372.428,"text":"Ouranos had promised that someone would pay."},{"id":"c9_2","s":372.728,"e":375.246,"text":"Now the Titans are paying."},{"id":"c10_1","s":375.746,"e":377.817,"text":"How deep is Tartarus?"},{"id":"c10_2","s":378.117,"e":380.215,"text":"Hesiod gives a measurement."},{"id":"c10_3","s":380.515,"e":388.185,"text":"A bronze anvil dropped from the sky takes nine days to reach the earth, and nine more to reach Tartarus."},{"id":"c11_1","s":388.985,"e":392.4,"text":"Poseidon fits gates of bronze to the prison."},{"id":"c11_2","s":392.7,"e":398.709,"text":"And in one passage, Hesiod has the three Hundred-Handers stay on as its guards."},{"id":"c12_1","s":399.209,"e":403.974,"text":"In Hesiod's telling, the freed prisoners are the key to the victory."},{"id":"c12_2","s":404.274,"e":408.034,"text":"For ten years, the young gods could not win alone."},{"id":"c12_3","s":408.334,"e":411.075,"text":"Ouranos had chained these three."},{"id":"c12_4","s":411.375,"e":415.955,"text":"Zeus and the gods freed them, fed them, and asked for their help."},{"id":"d1_1","s":416.855,"e":419.367,"text":"The Titans are locked away."},{"id":"d1_2","s":419.667,"e":422,"text":"But the poem is not finished."},{"id":"d2_1","s":422.5,"e":425.702,"text":"Gaia bears one more child, her youngest."},{"id":"d2_2","s":426.002,"e":427.206,"text":"Typhoeus."},{"id":"d2_3","s":427.506,"e":430.842,"text":"His father is Tartarus, the pit itself."},{"id":"d3_1","s":431.342,"e":439.574,"text":"Hesiod gives him a hundred snake heads, with dark, flickering tongues, and fire flashing from the eyes of every one."},{"id":"d4_1","s":440.074,"e":442.436,"text":"And a voice in every head."},{"id":"d4_2","s":442.736,"e":446.998,"text":"Sometimes it makes a sound the gods can understand."},{"id":"d4_3","s":447.298,"e":456.309,"text":"Sometimes it bellows like a bull, or roars like a lion, or yelps like puppies, or hisses until the mountains ring."},{"id":"d5_1","s":456.809,"e":460.9,"text":"That day, Hesiod says, something past help would have happened."},{"id":"d5_2","s":461.2,"e":467.559,"text":"Typhoeus would have ruled over gods and mortals, if Zeus had not noticed him in time."},{"id":"d6_1","s":468.059,"e":469.558,"text":"Zeus thunders."},{"id":"d6_2","s":469.858,"e":471.362,"text":"Olympus shakes."},{"id":"d6_3","s":471.662,"e":477.303,"text":"Hades trembles in the underworld, and the Titans tremble in Tartarus."},{"id":"d6_4","s":477.603,"e":482.668,"text":"Then Zeus leaps down from Olympus and burns every one of those heads."},{"id":"d7_1","s":483.168,"e":488.187,"text":"Typhoeus crashes down, burned and broken, and the earth groans."},{"id":"d7_2","s":488.487,"e":492.36,"text":"The ground itself melts, Hesiod says, like tin."},{"id":"d7_3","s":492.66,"e":495.868,"text":"And Zeus throws him into Tartarus."},{"id":"d8_1","s":496.368,"e":502.399,"text":"From Typhoeus come the storm winds, the ones that scatter ships and wreck sailors."},{"id":"d8_2","s":502.699,"e":508.763,"text":"Only three winds are not his: the north wind, the south wind, and the west wind."},{"id":"d9_1","s":509.663,"e":511.396,"text":"Now look at this jar."},{"id":"d10_1","s":512.296,"e":523.803,"text":"It is a water jar, a hydria, painted around five hundred and forty B.C. That is roughly a century and a half after Hesiod, by the usual dates."},{"id":"d10_2","s":524.103,"e":526.814,"text":"It sits in a museum in Munich."},{"id":"d11_1","s":527.314,"e":531.53,"text":"On the left, Zeus, with a thunderbolt raised in his hand."},{"id":"d11_2","s":531.83,"e":534.484,"text":"His name is painted beside him."},{"id":"d12_1","s":534.984,"e":537.089,"text":"On the right, the monster."},{"id":"d12_2","s":537.389,"e":543.129,"text":"The painter gave him a human face, huge wings, and a body of coiling snakes."},{"id":"d13_1","s":543.629,"e":548.487,"text":"Hesiod gave him a hundred snake heads, and never mentions wings."},{"id":"d14_1","s":548.987,"e":553.591,"text":"Maybe a painter simply has the curve of a pot to work with."},{"id":"e1_1","s":554.491,"e":557.975,"text":"Now the war is over, and the monster is gone."},{"id":"e2_1","s":558.475,"e":562.565,"text":"After the war, the gods urge Zeus to rule over them."},{"id":"e2_2","s":562.865,"e":565.626,"text":"And Gaia is the one who prompts them."},{"id":"e3_1","s":566.126,"e":570.192,"text":"Cronos took his father's place with a sickle in his hand."},{"id":"e3_2","s":570.492,"e":572.186,"text":"Zeus is urged."},{"id":"e4_1","s":572.686,"e":575.669,"text":"He divides the honors among the gods."},{"id":"e4_2","s":575.969,"e":577.835,"text":"Each one gets a share."},{"id":"e4_3","s":578.135,"e":579.772,"text":"And then he marries."},{"id":"e5_1","s":580.272,"e":582.704,"text":"His first wife is Metis."},{"id":"e5_2","s":583.004,"e":587.329,"text":"Hesiod says she is wiser than all gods and all mortals."},{"id":"e6_1","s":587.829,"e":591.14,"text":"Metis is about to give birth to Athena."},{"id":"e6_2","s":591.44,"e":597.52,"text":"That is when Zeus deceives her with cunning words, and puts her inside his own belly."},{"id":"e7_1","s":598.02,"e":601.983,"text":"He does it on the advice of Gaia and Ouranos."},{"id":"e7_2","s":602.283,"e":609.144,"text":"And the reason is stated plainly: so that no one else will ever take Zeus's place as king."},{"id":"e8_1","s":609.644,"e":613.342,"text":"Because Metis is destined to have two children."},{"id":"e8_2","s":613.642,"e":617.804,"text":"First, a daughter, as strong as her father, and as wise."},{"id":"e8_3","s":618.104,"e":623.919,"text":"And after her, a son with an overbearing spirit, who would be king of gods and men."},{"id":"e9_1","s":624.419,"e":627.506,"text":"Zeus does not wait for the son to grow up."},{"id":"e9_2","s":627.806,"e":630.705,"text":"He does not wait for the son to be born."},{"id":"e9_3","s":631.005,"e":632.842,"text":"He swallows the mother."},{"id":"e10_1","s":633.342,"e":636.17,"text":"Ouranos chained some of his children."},{"id":"e10_2","s":636.47,"e":638.461,"text":"Cronos swallowed his."},{"id":"e10_3","s":638.761,"e":642.446,"text":"Zeus swallowed the mother, before the son could exist."},{"id":"e11_1","s":642.946,"e":645.68,"text":"And notice who gives the advice."},{"id":"e11_2","s":646.03,"e":647.767,"text":"Gaia made the sickle."},{"id":"e11_3","s":648.117,"e":649.923,"text":"Gaia hid the baby."},{"id":"e11_4","s":650.273,"e":652.009,"text":"Gaia tricked Cronos."},{"id":"e11_5","s":652.359,"e":655.466,"text":"Gaia told the gods how to win the war."},{"id":"e11_6","s":655.816,"e":658.928,"text":"Gaia prompted them to make Zeus king."},{"id":"e11_7","s":659.278,"e":665.947,"text":"And Gaia, with Ouranos, tells Zeus how to make sure that nobody ever takes his place."},{"id":"e12_1","s":666.447,"e":671.714,"text":"Again and again in this poem, Gaia is behind the turns of power."},{"id":"f1_1","s":672.614,"e":675.12,"text":"Three rulers of the sky."},{"id":"f1_2","s":675.42,"e":678.377,"text":"The first was struck down by his son."},{"id":"f1_3","s":678.677,"e":681.769,"text":"The second was overthrown by his."},{"id":"f1_4","s":682.069,"e":687.234,"text":"The third made sure that the child who could do it to him was never born."},{"id":"f2_1","s":687.734,"e":690.348,"text":"Hesiod passes no judgment."},{"id":"f2_2","s":690.648,"e":695.233,"text":"He calls it a deception, done with cunning words, and moves on."},{"id":"f2_3","s":695.533,"e":698.57,"text":"And right after, Zeus marries again."},{"id":"f3_1","s":699.07,"e":701.332,"text":"But the poem leaves a puzzle."},{"id":"f3_2","s":701.632,"e":705.172,"text":"Metis was swallowed before Athena was born."},{"id":"f3_3","s":705.472,"e":712.936,"text":"And yet, a little further on, Hesiod says that Zeus himself gave birth to Athena, from his own head."},{"id":"f4_1","s":713.436,"e":719.93,"text":"Next time: the goddess who came out of her father's head, and what Hesiod says about her mother."}],"chapters":[{"t":40.274,"label":"I · THE FIRST RULER OF THE SKY"},{"t":145.723,"label":"II · THE STONE"},{"t":275.842,"label":"III · THE TEN-YEAR WAR"},{"t":416.855,"label":"IV · THE MONSTER"},{"t":509.663,"label":"V · THE JAR IN MUNICH"},{"t":554.491,"label":"VI · WHY ZEUS SWALLOWED METIS"},{"t":713.436,"label":""}],"shots":[{"t0":0,"t1":2.609,"bg":"night","sfx":[[0,"thud",1],[2.087,"gulp",1]],"pops":[0.15],"cut":true,"travel":false},{"t0":2.609,"t1":4.439,"bg":"night","sfx":[[2.975,"chime",0.7]],"pops":[2.609],"cut":false,"travel":false},{"t0":4.439,"t1":6.595,"bg":"night","sfx":[[4.439,"sting",0.8]],"pops":[4.439,4.939,5.039],"cut":false,"travel":false},{"t0":6.595,"t1":10.135,"bg":"parch","sfx":[[7.657,"whoosh",0.6]],"pops":[6.895],"cut":false,"travel":false},{"t0":10.135,"t1":14.02,"bg":"map","sfx":[[10.912,"thud",0.8]],"pops":[],"cut":false,"travel":true},{"t0":14.02,"t1":18.081,"bg":"storm","sfx":[[14.223,"roar",0.7]],"pops":[],"cut":false,"travel":false},{"t0":18.081,"t1":22.317,"bg":"olympus","sfx":[[19.352,"ding",0.8]],"pops":[18.081,18.481,18.581,18.681,18.781],"cut":false,"travel":false},{"t0":22.317,"t1":27.012,"bg":"chaos","sfx":[[22.317,"rewind",0.7]],"pops":[],"cut":f